    "id": "10497",
    "self": "https://simeoncalixte1990.atlassian.net/rest/api/3/issue/10497",
    "key": "AIN-174",
    "fields": {
      "summary": "[API Auth] Separate authentication orchestration, assertion validation and access policies",
      "description": {
        "type": "doc",
        "version": 1,
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": "Refactor EnsuredAPI/src/auth/authenticationHook after source review: separate signed assertion verification, account resolution/provisioning, shared route policies and domain scope validation. Keep a short orchestration hook and existing gateway exports for compatibility."
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": "Acceptance criteria:"
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": "1. Preserve supported public/guest/service/gateway access, exact raw URL and method binding, status/error codes and authorization check ordering. Unknown routes remain organization-restricted. No broadened organization or impersonation exceptions."
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": "2. Explicit dependency options and initialization-time integration configuration; discriminated verified identities; runtime validation for custom claims before attaching request context. Malformed signed scopes fail with existing denial codes; unexpected database errors remain server errors. No token/secret logging."
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": "3. Replace the large negative organization condition with named, reviewable policies; keep relationship scope validation with its domain. Attach request context only after authorization succeeds. Separate administrator provisioning and move account-link CLI under scripts, preserving npm auth:link."
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": "4. Regression matrix covers method/path/query boundaries, public versus invitation-authenticated endpoints, independent members, disabled/missing accounts, impersonation, relationship/participant scopes, service assertions and error precedence. Existing auth tests, typecheck and lint pass. Run isolated PostgreSQL authorization integration tests; record results and limitations."
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": "5. Document structure, compatibility, ticket/test mapping and deployment status in repository and TASK_HANDOFF.md. This task is local backend implementation and verification; no UI workflow, schema migration or deployment is requested."
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": "Scope map: this task is required. AIN-73/74 (identity consent/projection), AIN-84 (participation), AIN-138/143 (independent-member model/chat access) are affected-story traceability, not prerequisite deliveries. Their existing criteria, children and broader release obligations remain unchanged. No UI changes, so Storybook/screenshots are not applicable; use API/database evidence. Exclude AIN-1/12/13 releases, AIN-48/59 data-model expansion, AIN-79 filters and provider/staging delivery under AIN-137/138/143 because this structural refactor neither changes those workflows nor delivers their outcomes."
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
